# The Google OAuth client that cross-device sync signs in with.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the two values. `.env` is untracked on
# purpose: a client secret committed to a public repository gets flagged by
# GitHub's secret scanning, and Google is one of its partners — a leaked secret
# can be revoked automatically, which would break sync for everyone at once.
# It is not confidential otherwise; anyone can read it out of a released binary,
# and Google's own docs assume as much for this flow.
#
# To create one (about five minutes, no review, no fee):
#
#   1. console.cloud.google.com -> new project
#   2. APIs & Services -> Library -> enable "Google Drive API"
#   3. OAuth consent screen -> External. Add ONLY the scope
#      https://www.googleapis.com/auth/drive.file
#      It is classified non-sensitive, which is what exempts the app from
#      verification and from the 100-user cap. Any additional scope reinstates
#      both, silently.
#
#      Not drive.appdata, which would be the tidier hidden-folder equivalent:
#      Google's own docs list it as permitted for this flow, and the endpoint
#      rejects it. Measured, not assumed.
#   4. Publish the app to Production. In "Testing", refresh tokens expire after
#      seven days and sync dies every week.
#   5. Credentials -> Create OAuth client ID -> application type
#      "TVs and Limited Input devices". Not "Desktop app": the type is what
#      decides whether the device flow is allowed at all.
#
# Leaving these empty is supported. The app builds and runs; Settings says sync
# is unavailable in this build, and explains why.

WTA_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
WTA_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
